[Line "3...Qb6 4.Nc3 dxe4 5.Bc4 Nf6 6.fxe4 e5 7.dxe5 Ng4 8.Qe2"]
[Section "3...Qb6"]
[Summary "The queen sortie is Black's most awkward way to meet the Fantasy. White develops with Bc4 instead of recapturing, then regains the pawn and meets ...e5 with dxe5 and Qe2. Roughly equal."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 Qb6 { [%cal Rb6b2,Rb6d4] The queen hits b2 and d4 at once and stops White from simply
recapturing and enjoying the big centre. The third most popular reply (16%) and Black's best practical try. }
4. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3b5,Gc3e4] Develops and keeps the knight flexible. Black's choices are 4...dxe4 (70%), 4...e6
and 4...e5. } 4... dxe4 { [%cal Rd5e4] Takes the pawn so that White's pieces cannot use the e4 square. }
5. Bc4 { [%cal Gc4f7,Gc4b5] **Idea:** the study's choice (27% of the games). Instead of recapturing with the
f-pawn, White develops with tempo against f7 and keeps the option of taking on e4 with the knight. If Black
takes on f3, the knight recaptures with a lead in development. The main alternative is 5.fxe4 (56%), but it
leaves b2 and d4 as targets and 5...e5 is awkward. }
( 5. fxe4 { The natural recapture and the most played move. } 5... e5 6. Nf3 { The right way to meet ...e5:
develop and keep the tension. }
( 6. dxe5? { A mistake: the pawn grab hands the initiative to Black, who develops with tempo. Black's
bishop comes to e6 with ideas of ...Bc4 (stopping White from castling) and ...Bc5 against g1. }
6... Be6 7. Nf3 Nd7 { The extra pawn on e5 will fall, and the elite results favour Black (44 wins for Black,
34 for White). } )
6... exd4 7. Nxd4 { Equal in elite games (about 50%). Black has ...Nf6, ...h5 or ...Bc5; the Bc5 line
(8.Na4) scored badly for White (12 wins in 29 elite games), so know it before you walk in. } 7... Nf6 8. e5 )
5... Nf6 { [%cal Rf6e4] The most popular reply (two thirds). It defends the e4 pawn, develops and keeps the
choice of ...exf3 or ...e5. } 6. fxe4 { [%cal Ge4e5] Now the pawn is regained. } 6... e5 { [%cal Re5d4] Hits
the centre before White consolidates. } 7. dxe5 { [%cal Gd1d8] Takes the pawn; Black will win it back with
...Ng4 and ...Nxe5, but White gains the tempo Qe2 and Bb3. } 7... Ng4 { [%cal Rg4e5,Rg4f2] Attacks e5 and f2. }
8. Qe2 { [%cal Ge2g4,Ge2e5] Defends e5 and eyes the knight; 57 elite games with this move. } 8... Nxe5
{ **Position:** Black has regained the pawn; the bishop on c4 and the queen on e2 are both active, and the
knight on e5 hits the bishop, so White plays Bb3.

**Plan:** Bb3 and Nf3; the knights exchange on e5 or Black answers with ...Nbd7 and ...Bg4. The c4/b3 bishop
keeps f7 under pressure and the e-file pins are in the air for both sides.

**Verdict:** equal. Elite results are even (24 wins for White, 4 draws, 22 for Black). } 9. Bb3 Bg4 10. Nf3
Nbd7 *

[Line "3...Qb6 4.Nc3 dxe4 5.Bc4 exf3 6.Nxf3 Nf6 7.O-O"]
[Section "3...Qb6"]
[Summary "Black grabs the second pawn. White is a pawn down but develops a bishop and a knight with tempo and castles."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 Qb6 4. Nc3 dxe4 5. Bc4 exf3 { [%cal Rf3g2] Black accepts the second pawn. White is now
a pawn down. } 6. Nxf3 { [%cal Gf3e5,Gf3g5,Gc4f7] A real gambit: White has developed the knight and bishop
with a lead on the king, and Black's queen is the one piece that has moved. Elite games show an even
result (10 wins for White, 9 for Black in 20 games). } 6... Nf6 7. O-O { [%cal Gf1f7,Gf3g5] The king gets out and
the rook stands behind the f7 target. **Verdict:** a gambit for development: White has two pieces out and
the f-file; Black has the extra pawn and an undeveloped queenside. } *

[Line "3...Qb6 4.a4 a5 5.exd5 cxd5 6.Nc3 Nf6 7.Nb5"]
[Section "3...Qb6"]
[Summary "A quiet way to meet the queen: a4 stops ...Qb4+ and ...Qxb2 ideas, and after ...a5 the knight lands on b5. Rare, but it scores well."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 Qb6 4. a4 { [%cal Ga4a5,Ga4b5] Threatens a5, gaining time on the queen and taking b4 and
b6 away. It also supports a knight on b5. Played in only 5% of the games, but White scored 65% in them. }
4... a5 { [%cal Ra5b4] Stops a4-a5. The pawn on a5 weakens b5, which cannot be defended by a pawn any more. }
( 4... e5 { Black hits the centre. } 5. dxe5 dxe4 )
5. exd5 { [%cal Ge4d5] Removes Black's central pawn and lets the knight come to c3 and b5 at once. }
5... cxd5 6. Nc3 Nf6 7. Nb5 { [%cal Gb5c7,Gb5d6] **Idea:** the outpost on b5 is protected by the a4 pawn and Black
has no pawn to chase it. It hits c7 and d6 and ties Black's queen to c7. } 7... Bf5 { **Verdict:** pleasant for
White. The sample is small (nine games after 6...Nf6, with 7 wins for White), but the structure favours the
knight on b5 and White has a lead in development. } *

[Line "3...g6 4.Nc3 Bg7 5.Be3 Qb6 6.b3"]
[Section "3...g6"]
[Summary "Black fianchettoes and goes for the dark squares. White guards d4 with Be3, then stops the ...Qxb2 grab with b3."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 g6 { [%cal Rf8g7,Rg7d4] A Modern-style reply (9%): the fianchettoed bishop attacks the
centre from the long diagonal. Combined with ...Qb6 it makes the f3 pawn a liability, because the dark squares
around the white king are weak. } 4. Nc3 { Natural development; the pawn on d4 needs support. } 4... Bg7
5. Be3 { [%cal Ge3d4,Gd1d2] The main move (83%): the bishop guards d4 and prepares Qd2 and O-O-O. } 5... Qb6
{ [%cal Rb6b2,Rb6d4] The most popular reply (36%) hits b2 and d4 together. }
( 5... dxe4 { Also good for Black. } 6. fxe4 Nf6 7. Nf3 Ng4 8. Bg1 { The bishop has to retreat to g1. It looks
awkward, but 8.Bg1 is the book move (33 of 38 elite games) and scores about even (14 wins, 3 draws, 16
losses). } )
( 5... Nf6 6. e5 Nfd7 7. f4 { The French structure again, with the extra Bg7 for Black. Slightly worse for
White in the sample (15 wins in 39 games). } )
6. b3 { [%cal Gb2b3] Closes the diagonal so the b2 pawn is no longer a target. The best-scoring reply in the
elite sample (21 wins, 2 draws and 8 losses in 31 games). The price is a weakened c3 and the long diagonal
that Black's Bg7 eyes. }
( 6. Qd2 { The study's move. } 6... Qxb2 7. Rb1 Qa3 8. exd5 Nf6 9. dxc6 Nxc6 { A pawn is sacrificed for
activity: the black queen on a3 is out of play for a while, but White cannot castle short, the dark squares
around the king are weak and the f3 pawn is badly placed. The study's author did not like it, and the elite
results agree: 43 wins for White, 54 for Black in 103 games. } )
( 6. Rb1 { Another way to cover b2. } 6... e5 7. Nge2 )
6... e5 { [%cal Re5d4] Black's main reply (about 57%): he breaks the centre before White consolidates. }
7. Nge2 { [%cal Gg1e2,Ge2g3] **Verdict:** the knight comes out and covers g3, c3 and d4. White gets a
playable position with an extra tempo in the centre, but it is no better than equal, and the Bg7 on the long
diagonal is a constant reminder of what the f3 pawn costs. } *

[Line "3...e5 4.dxe5 Bc5 5.Nc3 Qb6 6.Na4 Qa5+ 7.c3 Bxg1 8.Rxg1 dxe4"]
[Section "Other third moves"]
[Summary "Black offers a pawn to open the diagonal to g1. White takes it, answers ...Bc5-Qb6 with Na4, and stands up fine after the trade of bishop for knight."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 e5 { [%cal Re5d4] A pawn offer (5%): Black opens lines against the weakened diagonal. }
4. dxe5 { [%cal Gd1d8] Takes the pawn. 4.exd5 is the alternative but scores worse. } 4... Bc5 { [%cal Rc5g1,Rc5f2]
Aims at g1 and f2: the pawn on f3 has left the knight's guard. } 5. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3d5] Develops and eyes d5. }
5... Qb6 { [%cal Rb6b2,Rb6f2] Doubles on f2 and hits b2. } 6. Na4 { [%cal Ga4b6,Ga4c5] The only move: it attacks
the queen and the bishop at once. } 6... Qa5+ { [%cal Ra5e1] The queen checks and keeps an eye on the knight. }
( 6... Bf2+ { Black cashes in the bishop sacrifice first. } 7. Ke2 Qd4 8. Qxd4 { The king steps to e2 and Black
swaps queens. Elite results are level (43 wins for Black, 39 for White in 84 games), and the endgame is not
a simple one. You are better off steering the game to the 6...Qa5+ line. } )
7. c3 { [%cal Gc3b4] Blocks the check. } 7... Bxg1 { Black takes the knight, since White cannot castle short
anyway. } 8. Rxg1 dxe4 { **Verdict:** equal, with 9.f4 to support the e5 pawn. White has lost the right to
castle, but Black has lost his dark-squared bishop and his queen is placed on a5; elite results are about
level (52 wins for White, 40 for Black in 100 games). } *

[Line "3...Nf6 and the rare third moves"]
[Section "Other third moves"]
[Summary "3...Nf6 4.e5 Nfd7 5.f4 is the Steinitz French again. Rare replies like ...a6, ...Nd7, ...h5 and ...Qa5+ are met by 4.Nc3."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 Nf6 { [%cal Rf6e4] Challenges e4 at once (3% of the games). }
( 3... a6 { A waiting move. } 4. Nc3 { The most natural: develop and keep the centre. } 4... dxe4 5. fxe4 )
( 3... Nd7 { Prepares ...e5 and ...Ngf6. } 4. Nc3 e6 5. Be3 { A French structure with the knight on d7: the
Bb4 line is Black's best, but White is happy with the extra tempo of f3. } )
( 3... h5 { A strange move. } 4. Nc3 e6 { Black wants ...h4. } 5. Be3 )
( 3... Qa5+ { A sortie before ...dxe4. } 4. Nc3 dxe4 5. fxe4 e5 6. Nf3 )
4. e5 { [%cal Ge5f6] Gains a tempo, and the f3 pawn gets to move to f4. } 4... Nfd7 { [%cal Rd7e5] Attacks the
e5 pawn. } 5. f4 { [%cal Gf3f4] Supports e5 and sets up the Steinitz chain. } 5... e6 6. Nc3 { **Verdict:** a
Steinitz French again; after 6...c5 7.Nf3 Nc6 8.Be3 the position is the one of the 4...Nf6 line. 4...Ng8 is
also played (a third of the games) and loses time: White keeps the space. } 6... c5 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. Be3 *
